186133460 1145 "To find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nc_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} &= \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}3c_{1} + 2c_{2} \\\\ c_{1} - c_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{3c_{1} + 2c_{2} = 7; c_{1} - c_{2} =-1\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $2$ and adding the result to the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n5c_1= 5 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad c_1 = 1\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n1 - c_2 = -1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad c_2 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= 1 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + 2 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}e^{-t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535571 false] [17592186133460 1145 "To find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=7 \\\\ {c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $2$ and adding the result to the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n5c_1= 5 \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nc_1 = 1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n1 - c_2 = -1\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nc_2 = 2.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535571 true] [17592186133534 1100 "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/e3f6d6e26983db7b894d0bb2ba09e66fdc55edecb0d9bc9d8a35821449707b7e.png)\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=2$ and $\\lambda_2=-3.$*" 13194139535571 true] [17592186133534 1100 "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 3x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 6x_{1}(t) - 4x_{2}(t)$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=2$ and $\\lambda_2=-3$.*" 13194139535571 false] [17592186133534 1145 "Let's construct the matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nfor the given system\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ x_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n$\\lambda_1= 2 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\lambda_2= -3$.\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.For $\\lambda_1 =2$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we get an eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$For $\\lambda_2 =-3$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we get an eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= c_{1}v_{1}e^{λ_{1}t} + c_{2}v_{2}e^{λ_{2}t} \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{2t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}e^{-3t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution and use the initial values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nc_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} + c_{2} \\\\ c_{1} + 6c_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{c_{1} + c_{2} = 5; c_{1} + 6c_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n5c_{2} =-5⟹c_{2} =-1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $c_2 = -1$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nc_{1} - 1 = 5⟹c_{1} = 6\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= 6\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{2t} - \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}e^{-3t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535571 false] [17592186133534 1145 "Let's construct the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ for the given system\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/4790200622ff45c885147ca0c897eefcbb4bdcefe10d67b8960cc167c3b2cccf.png)\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1= 2\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\lambda_2= -3.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1 =2,$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/10bbcec99a48d331c21836dc104a1db2db241e50a534baf0db23c138168c5663.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/c2bff2faa79d780c5f89b28e72468c84a388d50e8c566b80e5d5677f90a5e3af.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2 =-3,$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/704493d5ac4f48c88a9c521c4e43c41acfd80b6d6959237944fbc0d638e6012f.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/a77312d93a804e69dabb70aff5bc7bbf9798781c728dfc50fff1440da86b5b5c.png)\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2,$ we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution and use the initial values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ {c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{c}_{2}=-5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $c_2 = -1$ into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-1=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =6[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535571 true] [17592186133535 1100 "Let $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=-2$ be eigenvalues of the $2\\times 2$ matrix  $A$ that correspond to eigenvectors \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/15d8764ce12b985a991ca9a0704ba037ccc29fa488a3f23ee643688ceebae60c.png) \nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ and that $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/88cd1c87501899a5d6b49ce3fd1678fe9485300a2da638fb496f2c80b298ea86.png)\nfind the position of the particle after $2$ seconds." 13194139535571 true] [17592186133535 1100 "Let $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=-2$ be eigenvalues of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ that correspond to eigenvectors\n$\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ and that $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nfind the position of the particle after $2$ seconds." 13194139535571 false] [17592186133535 1145 "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}, {\\color{blue}\\lambda_2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$, the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}, \\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$, we substitute $t=0$ into the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nc_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}6c_{1} + c_{2} \\\\ -2c_{1} - 3c_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{6c_{1} + c_{2} =-11; - 2c_{1} - 3c_{2} = 1\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $3$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n16c_1 = -32 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad c_1=-2\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-2\\cdot(-2) -3c_2 = 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad c_2=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution for the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= -2 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} + 1 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $\\mathbf x(2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(2) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}e^{3 \\cdot 2} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-12e^{6} + e^{-4} \\\\ 4e^{6} - 3e^{-4}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535571 false] [17592186133535 1145 "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}, {\\color{blue}\\lambda_2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}, \\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2,$ we substitute $t=0$ into the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ -2{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=-11 \\\\ -2{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $3$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n16c_1 = -32\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nc_1=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-2\\cdot(-2) -3c_2 = 1\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nc_2=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution for the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $\\mathbf x(2)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2) & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3\\cdot 2}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12{e}^{6}+{e}^{-4} \\\\ 4{e}^{6}-3{e}^{-4}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535571 true] [17592186133877 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355643 13194139535571 true] [17592186133877 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355653 13194139535571 true] [17592186133877 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355662 13194139535571 true] [17592186133877 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355671 13194139535571 true] [1759218